# DESCRIPTION
# Using movies genres, perform the initial steps of a two-sample t-test.
# ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('WeBWorK')
## DBchapter('Nursing-Med Calc')
## DBsection('Problem')
## KEYWORDS('null hypothesis', 'alternative hypothesis', 'two-sample test')
## Author('Catherine Buell')
## Institution('Fitchburg State')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",   'PGML.pl',
    'randomPerson.pl', "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

Context("Numeric")->flags->set(
    tolerance => 0.02,
    tolType   => "absolute",
);

@opts = ('\(<\)', '\(>\)', '\(=\)', '\(\neq\)');
$dd1  = DropDown([@opts], 2, useHTMLSelect => 0);
$dd2  = DropDown([@opts], 0, useHTMLSelect => 0);

do {
    $n1 = random(20, 40);
    $n2 = random(20, 40);
} until $n1 != $n2;

do {
    $x1 = random(4.5, 6.5, 0.01);
    $x2 = random(5.5, 7.8, 0.01);
} until $x2 - $x1 > 0.05;

$p = randomPerson();

BEGIN_PGML

[$p] wants to know whether romance movies are more highly rated on average than 
action movies on a movie review website. [$p->They] [$p->verb('take')] a 
random sample of [$n1] action movies (group 1) and [$p->verb('find')] a 
sample mean rating of [$x1] on a 0-10 scale, and [$p->they] [$p->verb('take')] 
a random sample of [$n2] romance movies (group 2) and [$p->verb('find')] a 
sample mean rating of [$x2].

a) The null hypothesis would be: [`H_0`]: [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd1} [`\mu_{2}`]

b) The alternative hypothesis would be: [`H_a`]:  [`\mu_{1}`] [_]{$dd2}   [`\mu_{2}`]

c) What is [`n_1`]? [_]{$n1}

d) What is [`n_2`]? [_]{$n2}

e) What is [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2`] (to two decimal places)? [_]{Real($x1-$x2) }

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) The null hypothesis is the neutral relationship and that is [`\mu_1=\mu_2`].

b) Since [$p] wants to know if romance movies rank higher than action movies, 
then if [`\mu_1`] is the mean of the action movies and [`\mu_2`] is the mean 
of the rating of the romance movies, then [$p->they] [$p->verb('want')] to 
test that [`\mu_1 < \mu_2`], so this is the alternative hypothesis.

c) [`n_1`] is the number of sample in group 1 or [`[$n1]`].

d) [`n_2`] is the number of sample in group 2 or [`[$n2]`].

e) The problem states that [`\bar{x}_1=[$x1]`] and [`\bar{x}_2 = [$x2]`] so 
    [`\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2 = [$x1-$x2] `].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
